> Yes, broadly right — with one strong caveat:
> 
> ## 1. Session memory
> I would **not** ignore type A entirely.
> 
> If your conversations are always short enough to fit comfortably in context, then fine: the raw conversation can serve as session memory.
> 
> But in practice, even for medium-length conversations, you usually want a **session-state layer** that is not just “the whole conversation”:
> - current goal
> - key constraints
> - decisions made
> - unresolved questions
> - tool results
> - draft plan / next steps
> 
> Why? Because:
> - raw transcripts are noisy
> - retrieval from raw turns is worse than retrieval from distilled state
> - long conversations will eventually exceed practical context
> - even before that, summarization improves reliability
> 
> So I’d say:
> 
> - **short term:** use the full conversation as session memory
> - **design-wise:** still reserve a place in your architecture for a `SessionState` or conversation summary object
> 
> You can defer implementing it, but I wouldn’t conceptually remove it.
