**assistant**:
> Yes. Below is a **class/function-prototype design** for a Python memory system built around:
> 
> - `ConversationMemory`
> - `LongTermMemory`
> 
> with support for:
> 
> - multiple users
> - test isolation
> - pluggable storage/indexing
> - your existing `sqlalchemy` and optionally `whoosh`
> - unit tests using temporary or in-memory backends
> - future extension into more agentic task memory
> 
> I’ll keep this at the **prototype / interface / skeleton** level, but concrete enough that you can start implementing directly.
> 
> ---
> 
> # Design goals
> 
> 1. **Clear split**
>    - `ConversationMemory`: per-conversation, ephemeral
>    - `LongTermMemory`: persistent across conversations
> 
> 2. **Multi-user**
>    - all memory keyed by `user_id`
>    - optional `agent_id` / `persona_id` later if needed
> 
> 3. **Testable**
>    - injectable backends
>    - in-memory or temp-db implementations
>    - no coupling to production memory
> 
> 4. **Storage-agnostic**
>    - SQLAlchemy for canonical storage
>    - Whoosh optional for text/keyword retrieval
>    - later embeddings/vector retrieval can be added behind interfaces
> 
> 5. **Structured memory**
>    - conversation summaries
>    - facts/preferences
>    - tasks/projects
>    - source/provenance metadata
> 
> ---
> 
> # High-level architecture
> 
> ```text
> User turn loop
>    |
>    v
> ConversationMemory
>    |  \
>    |   \--> asks LongTermMemory for relevant prior memory
>    |
>    --> returns prompt block / summary / state
>    |
> Conversation ends
>    |
>    v
> LongTermMemory.post_process_conversation(...)
>    --> extract candidate memories
>    --> dedupe/update/store
>    --> index summaries/documents
> ```
> 
> ---
> 
> # Core domain models
> 
> These can be dataclasses or Pydantic models. I’d suggest Pydantic if you already use it; otherwise dataclasses are fine.
> 
> ## Shared enums / literals
> 
> ```python
> from __future__ import annotations
> from dataclasses import dataclass, field
> from datetime import datetime
> from typing import Optional, Sequence, Any, Literal
> 
> MemoryKind = Literal[
>     "conversation_summary",
>     "episode",
>     "fact",
>     "preference",
>     "task",
>     "project",
>     "artifact",
> ]
> 
> TaskStatus = Literal["open", "in_progress", "blocked", "done", "cancelled"]
> Role = Literal["system", "user", "assistant", "tool"]
> ```
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Conversation message
> 
> ```python
> @dataclass
> class Message:
>     role: Role
>     content: str
>     created_at: datetime
>     metadata: dict[str, Any] = field(default_factory=dict)
> ```
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Conversation transcript
> 
> ```python
> @dataclass
> class ConversationTranscript:
>     conversation_id: str
>     user_id: str
>     started_at: datetime
>     ended_at: Optional[datetime]
>     messages: list[Message]
>     metadata: dict[str, Any] = field(default_factory=dict)
> 
>     def add_message(self, message: Message) -> None:
>         self.messages.append(message)
> ```
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Memory items
> 
> I recommend a single generic memory record at the interface level, even if internally you use specialized tables.
> 
> ```python
> @dataclass
> class MemoryRecord:
>     memory_id: str
>     user_id: str
>     kind: MemoryKind
>     content: str
>     summary: Optional[str]
>     created_at: datetime
>     updated_at: Optional[datetime] = None
> 
>     # optional scoping
>     conversation_id: Optional[str] = None
>     project_id: Optional[str] = None
>     task_id: Optional[str] = None
>     subject: Optional[str] = None
> 
>     # quality / lifecycle
>     importance: float = 0.5
>     confidence: float = 0.7
>     valid_from: Optional[datetime] = None
>     valid_until: Optional[datetime] = None
>     is_active: bool = True
> 
>     # provenance / retrieval
>     source: str = "conversation"
>     tags: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)
>     metadata: dict[str, Any] = field(default_factory=dict)
> ```
> 
> ---
> 
> ## User profile
> 
> This is a distilled view, not necessarily the authoritative store.
> 
> ```python
> @dataclass
> class UserProfile:
>     user_id: str
>     display_name: Optional[str] = None
>     preferences: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)
>     facts: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)
>     active_projects: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)
>     active_tasks: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)
>     updated_at: Optional[datetime] = None
> ```
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Task record
> 
> ```python
> @dataclass
> class TaskRecord:
>     task_id: str
>     user_id: str
>     title: str
>     status: TaskStatus
>     goal: Optional[str] = None
>     plan: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)
>     current_step: Optional[str] = None
>     blockers: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)
>     artifacts: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)
>     created_at: datetime = field(default_factory=datetime.utcnow)
>     updated_at: datetime = field(default_factory=datetime.utcnow)
>     metadata: dict[str, Any] = field(default_factory=dict)
> ```
> 
> ---
> 
> # Retrieval and prompt assembly models
> 
> ## Long-term retrieval result
> 
> ```python
> @dataclass
> class RetrievedMemoryBundle:
>     user_id: str
>     query: str
>     profile: Optional[UserProfile]
>     relevant_memories: list[MemoryRecord]
>     active_tasks: list[TaskRecord]
>     generated_summary_block: str
> ```
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Conversation state snapshot
> 
> ```python
> @dataclass
> class ConversationState:
>     conversation_id: str
>     user_id: str
>     running_summary: str = ""
>     open_questions: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)
>     current_goals: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)
>     constraints: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)
>     important_points: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)
>     last_updated: Optional[datetime] = None
> ```
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Post-processing result
> 
> ```python
> @dataclass
> class PostProcessResult:
>     conversation_id: str
>     user_id: str
>     stored_memory_ids: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)
>     updated_task_ids: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)
>     updated_profile: bool = False
>     candidate_memories: list[MemoryRecord] = field(default_factory=list)
>     notes: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)
> ```
> 
> ---
> 
> # Storage interfaces
> 
> These interfaces are the key to testability.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Canonical persistent store
> 
> This is where SQLAlchemy fits naturally.
> 
> ```python
> from typing import Protocol
> 
> class MemoryStore(Protocol):
>     def create_user_if_missing(self, user_id: str) -> None: ...
> 
>     def save_memory(self, memory: MemoryRecord) -> str: ...
>     def update_memory(self, memory: MemoryRecord) -> None: ...
>     def get_memory(self, user_id: str, memory_id: str) -> Optional[MemoryRecord]: ...
>     def list_memories(
>         self,
>         user_id: str,
>         *,
>         kind: Optional[MemoryKind] = None,
>         active_only: bool = True,
>         limit: int = 100,
>     ) -> list[MemoryRecord]: ...
> 
>     def find_memories_by_filters(
>         self,
>         user_id: str,
>         *,
>         kind: Optional[MemoryKind] = None,
>         conversation_id: Optional[str] = None,
>         project_id: Optional[str] = None,
>         task_id: Optional[str] = None,
>         tags: Optional[Sequence[str]] = None,
>         active_only: bool = True,
>         limit: int = 100,
>     ) -> list[MemoryRecord]: ...
> 
>     def save_conversation(self, transcript: ConversationTranscript) -> None: ...
>     def get_conversation(self, user_id: str, conversation_id: str) -> Optional[ConversationTranscript]: ...
> 
>     def save_user_profile(self, profile: UserProfile) -> None: ...
>     def get_user_profile(self, user_id: str) -> Optional[UserProfile]: ...
> 
>     def save_task(self, task: TaskRecord) -> str: ...
>     def update_task(self, task: TaskRecord) -> None: ...
>     def get_task(self, user_id: str, task_id: str) -> Optional[TaskRecord]: ...
>     def list_tasks(
>         self,
>         user_id: str,
>         *,
>         status: Optional[TaskStatus] = None,
>         limit: int = 100,
>     ) -> list[TaskRecord]: ...
> 
>     def delete_memory(self, user_id: str, memory_id: str) -> bool: ...
> ```
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Search index interface
> 
> This is where Whoosh can slot in.
> 
> ```python
> class MemoryIndex(Protocol):
>     def index_memory(self, memory: MemoryRecord) -> None: ...
>     def remove_memory(self, memory_id: str) -> None: ...
> 
>     def search(
>         self,
>         user_id: str,
>         query: str,
>         *,
>         kinds: Optional[Sequence[MemoryKind]] = None,
>         limit: int = 10,
>     ) -> list[str]:
>         """Return memory_ids in ranked order."""
>         ...
> 
>     def index_conversation_summary(
>         self,
>         user_id: str,
>         conversation_id: str,
>         summary: str,
>         metadata: Optional[dict[str, Any]] = None,
>     ) -> None: ...
> ```
> 
> If Whoosh already indexes conversation summaries, you can initially use it only for:
> - prior conversation summaries
> - memory summaries / facts
> 
> Later you can add embedding retrieval without changing `LongTermMemory`.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## Extraction / summarization model interface
> 
> This wraps LLM calls for:
> - turn summary
> - conversation summary
> - memory extraction
> - profile updates
> - task extraction
> 
> ```python
> class MemoryModel(Protocol):
>     def summarize_conversation(
>         self,
>         transcript: ConversationTranscript,
>         existing_summary: Optional[str] = None,
>     ) -> str: ...
> 
>     def summarize_turn_delta(
>         self,
>         transcript: ConversationTranscript,
>         previous_state: ConversationState,
>     ) -> ConversationState: ...
> 
>     def build_retrieval_query(
>         self,
>         recent_messages: Sequence[Message],
>         conversation_state: ConversationState,
>     ) -> str: ...
> 
>     def extract_candidate_memories(
>         self,
>         transcript: ConversationTranscript,
>         conversation_summary: str,
>     ) -> list[MemoryRecord]: ...
> 
>     def merge_profile(
>         self,
>         existing_profile: Optional[UserProfile],
>         candidate_memories: Sequence[MemoryRecord],
>     ) -> UserProfile: ...
> 
>     def build_prompt_memory_block(
>         self,
>         profile: Optional[UserProfile],
>         memories: Sequence[MemoryRecord],
>         tasks: Sequence[TaskRecord],
>         conversation_state: ConversationState,
>     ) -> str: ...
> ```
> 
> This is the main seam for mocking in tests.
> 
