> # Notes on `store_sqlalchemy.py`
> 
> A few practical comments.
> 
> ## 1. ID generation
> Right now I used simple timestamp/counter IDs. That is fine for a prototype, but I’d probably replace them with:
> 
> ```python
> import uuid
> uuid.uuid4().hex
> ```
> 
> for both memory and task IDs.
> 
> So:
> 
> ```python
> def _next_memory_id(self) -> str:
>     return f"mem-{uuid.uuid4().hex}"
> ```
> 
> and same for tasks.
> 
> That is cleaner and safer.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 2. JSON columns
> I used SQLAlchemy `JSON` for:
> - profile lists
> - memory tags / metadata
> - conversation metadata
> - task plan/blockers/artifacts/metadata
> 
> This works well in:
> - SQLite
> - Postgres
> 
> If you’re on older SQLite setups, JSON may just be stored as text behind the scenes, which is still okay for this use case.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 3. Tags filtering
> In `find_memories_by_filters`, I filtered tags in Python after fetching rows.
> 
> That’s intentional for simplicity, because portable SQL querying over JSON arrays is annoying. If you later move heavily to Postgres, you can optimize this.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 4. Conversation replacement behavior
> `save_conversation` currently replaces an existing conversation with the same `conversation_id`.
> 
> That’s good for deterministic tests and “save final transcript at end” behavior.
> 
> If you want append-in-place support later, you can add:
> - `create_conversation(...)`
> - `append_message(...)`
> - `finalize_conversation(...)`
> 
> But for the current architecture, saving the whole transcript is simpler.
> 
> ---
> 
> # You also asked whether I had started on `store_inmemory.py`
> 
> Yes, but only as a partial sketch earlier. The version above is the proper full prototype file.
