> 
> ## Best practice
> Track whether each source conversation has already been ingested, using a stable key like:
> 
> - `user_id`
> - `conversation_id` (your basename is fine if unique)
> - optionally a source fingerprint:
>   - file mtime
>   - file size
>   - content hash
> 
> Then skip if already ingested.
> 
> ---
> 
> # Simple solution: check for existing conversation
> Since `post_process_conversation()` already calls `store.save_conversation(transcript)`, and your store can retrieve conversations by `conversation_id`, the easiest first step is:
> 
> ```python
> existing = store.get_conversation(user_id, basename)
> if existing is not None:
>     continue
> ```
> 
> So:
> 
> ```python
> for file_path in logdir.glob('*.log'):
>     basename = os.path.basename(file_path)
> 
>     existing = store.get_conversation(user_id, basename)
>     if existing is not None:
>         continue
> 
>     integration_long_term.ingest_conversation(
>         long_term,
>         user_id=user_id,
>         conversation_id=basename,
>         messages=chatwrap.load_log(None, file_path),
>         started_at=datetime.strptime(basename[4:23], "%Y-%m-%d_%H-%M-%S"),
>         ended_at=datetime.fromtimestamp(os.path.getmtime(file_path)),
>     )
> ```
> 
> This is probably enough if:
> - each log file corresponds to exactly one conversation
> - `basename` is stable and unique
> - you never want to re-import changed files under the same name
> 
> ---
> 
> # Better solution: store ingestion metadata
> The issue with only checking `get_conversation()` is that:
> - if ingestion failed halfway through, you might have partial memory written
> - if the file changes later, you may want to reingest
> - if transcript saved but post-processing failed, the existence check may give a false sense of completion
> 
> So the more robust approach is to track an **ingestion record**.
> 
> ## Add metadata to the conversation
> At minimum, add to transcript metadata:
> 
> ```python
> metadata={
>     "source_file": str(file_path),
>     "source_mtime": os.path.getmtime(file_path),
>     "source_size": os.path.getsize(file_path),
>     "ingested_at": datetime.utcnow().isoformat(),
> }
> ```
> 
> Then you can compare current file metadata to stored metadata.
> 
> ---
> 
> # Most robust approach: explicit ingestion table
> If you want this to be production-solid, add a table like:
> 
> - `user_id`
> - `source_id` (e.g. basename or full path)
> - `source_mtime`
> - `source_size`
> - `status` (`pending`, `complete`, `failed`)
> - `conversation_id`
> - `last_error`
> - `updated_at`
> 
> Then your loop becomes:
> 
> 1. check ingestion record
> 2. if complete and unchanged, skip
> 3. if failed or changed, retry
> 4. mark complete only after successful `post_process_conversation`
> 
> This is the real best practice.
