> Yes — I agree. That’s better.
> 
> A hard switch on “low-information query” is a crude hack. A **prior + evidence** framing is cleaner and more robust:
> 
> - every memory has a **base relevance prior**
> - the query adds **likelihood evidence**
> - vague inputs produce little update, so the prior dominates
> - specific inputs shift results toward query-matched memories
> 
> That is exactly the right direction.
> 
> ## Conceptual scoring model
> 
> For each memory, compute something like:
> 
> \[
> \text{score} = \alpha \cdot \text{prior} + \beta \cdot \text{query\_match} + \gamma \cdot \text{recency} + \delta \cdot \text{kind\_bonus}
> \]
> 
> Where the **prior** can be built mostly from:
> - `importance`
> - `confidence`
> 
> and optionally:
> - kind stability (`fact`, `preference`, `project` tend to be more generally useful)
> - task freshness
> - correction bonus
> 
> So yes: instead of “if query is hello, use defaults”, do:
> - `query_match` is near zero for “Hello.”
> - then ranking is mostly prior-driven
> 
> That’s much nicer.
